          "question_type": "Statement Elimination",
          "question": "With reference to Indian Monsoon & Retreating Northeast Monsoon, consider the following statements:\n1. While most of India receives over 75% of its annual rainfall from the Southwest Monsoon (June–September), the southern coastal districts of Andhra Pradesh (Nellore, Tirupati, Prakasam) and Rayalaseema receive a substantial portion (30–50%) of their annual rainfall from the Retreating / Northeast Monsoon (October–December).\n2. Coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema receive 100% of their rainfall only from Western Disturbances in January and zero rainfall from October to December.\n\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?",
          "options": [
            "1 only",
            "2 only",
            "Both 1 and 2",
            "Neither 1 nor 2"
          ],
          "correct_index": 0,
          "explanation": "Only Statement 1 is correct.\n• Statement 1 is correct: While most of India receives over 75% of its annual rainfall from the Southwest Monsoon (June–September), the southern coastal districts of Andhra Pradesh (Nellore, Tirupati, Prakasam) and Rayalaseema receive a substantial portion (30–50%) of their annual rainfall from the Retreating / Northeast Monsoon (October–December).\n• Statement 2 is incorrect because: During the Northeast Monsoon, winds blowing over the warm Bay of Bengal pick up moisture and frequently interact with tropical cyclonic depressions to bring heavy post-monsoon rainfall to the Coromandel coast. (The claim that 'Coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema receive 100% of their rainfall only from Western Disturbances in January and zero rainfall from October to December.' is factually false).",
