          "question": "Consider the following statements regarding recent constitutional rulings on legislative immunity and the Tenth Schedule:\n1. In 'Sita Soren vs. Union of India' (2024), a 7-Judge Constitution Bench of the Supreme Court overruled the 1998 'P.V. Narasimha Rao' judgment and held that MPs and MLAs do NOT enjoy immunity under Articles 105(2) and 194(2) from criminal prosecution for accepting bribes to cast a vote or ask a question in the Legislature.\n2. According to the Supreme Court, the offence of bribery under the Prevention of Corruption Act is complete the moment an legislator accepts the illegal gratification, regardless of whether they subsequently vote or speak in the House as agreed.\n3. Under the Tenth Schedule of the Constitution, an independent member of a House becomes disqualified only if they join a political party AFTER the expiry of six months from the date of taking their seat.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?",
