          "question_type": "Pair Matching",
          "question": "Consider the following ancient Buddhist sites in Andhra Pradesh and their distinctive archaeological features:\n1. Bhattiprolu (Bapatla District) — Yielded early Brahmi inscriptions on crystal/stone reliquaries (c. 3rd century BCE) mentioning King Kuberaka and the 'Gosthi' (guild/committee) system.\n2. Nagarjunakonda / Vijayapuri (Palnadu District) — Capital of the Ikshvaku dynasty featuring apsidal chaityagrihas, an amphitheatre with acoustic properties, and a Mahachaitya whose relics were salvaged onto an island museum before submergence by the Nagarjunasagar Dam.\n3. Salihundam (Srikakulam District) — Located on the southern bank of the Vamsadhara River, showing continuity from Theravada to Vajrayana Buddhism (with sculptures of Tara and Marichi).\n4. Thotlakonda and Bavikonda — Rock-cut cave sites located in Kurnool district on the banks of the Tungabhadra River.\n\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?",
