          "question_type": "Multi-Statement Analysis",
          "question": "Consider the following statements regarding the administrative and agrarian institutions of medieval Andhra dynasties:\n1. Under the Kakatiyas of Warangal, the 'Nayankara' system assigned revenue-yielding villages to military commanders (Nayakas) in lieu of maintaining stipulated contingents of foot soldiers, horses, and elephants for the royal army.\n2. The 'Dashabandha' (Dasavanda) tenure prevalent during the Kakatiya and Vijayanagara periods was a punitive religious tax levied on non-agriculturists.\n3. The Motupalli Abhaya Shasana (1244 CE), issued by Kakatiya ruler Ganapatideva (and later renewed by Rudramadevi and Reddi king Anavota Reddi), guaranteed safety and fixed customs duties for foreign maritime merchants trading at Motupalli port in Prakasam/Bapatla coast.\n\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?",
          "options": [
            "1 and 2 only",
            "1 and 3 only",
            "2 and 3 only",
            "1, 2 and 3"
          ],
          "correct_index": 1,
          "explanation": "Statements 1 and 3 are correct; Statement 2 is incorrect.\n• Statement 1 is correct: Prataparudra's reign systematized the Nayankara military-fiscal assignment system, which was later expanded into the Amara-Nayaka system under Vijayanagara.\n• Statement 2 is incorrect: 'Dashabandha' (Dasavanda) was a tax-free land or revenue grant (typically 1/10th of the produce/ayacut) awarded to individuals who constructed, repaired, or maintained irrigation tanks and canals.\n• Statement 3 is correct: The Motupalli Charter (Abhaya Shasana of 1244 CE) abolished arbitrary confiscation of shipwrecked cargo and fixed fair tariffs.",
